==Low German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|osx|nds}} {{m|osx|stelan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|nds}} {{m|gem-pro|*stelaną}}.

===Verb===
{{head|nds|verb|third-person singular simple present|stehlt|past tense|stohl|past participle|stahlen|auxiliary verb|hebben}}
# {{lb|nds|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[steal]]
# {{lb|nds|reflexive}} to [[skulk]], to [[move]] [[secretly]]

====Usage notes====
As in High German, the person from whom the thing is stolen is given in the oblique case. Thus, “''He hett '''mi''' en Book stahlen''” means: “He stole a book from me.” This is quite contrary to English usage, where “He stole me a book” would be the equivalent of Low German: “''He hett en Book '''för''' mi stahlen.''”

====Conjugation====
{{nds-conj-st|class=4|stehl|stehl|stohl|stohl|stahl}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|nds|roven}}

